{
"dur": 63.949,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 11.021,
"text": "Taipei, June 2024, just after midnight. It's Computex week, and the whole AI industry is in town. At a bar, a table of tech executives is winding down.",
"end": 11.296
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.296,
"vo_start": 11.571,
"vo_end": 18.453,
"text": "One of them is a Chinese businesswoman named Alice Huang. She bets them she can get Jensen Huang, the CEO of Nvidia, to come.",
"end": 18.728
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 18.728,
"vo_start": 19.003,
"vo_end": 28.431,
"text": "This week he can't walk down a street in Taipei without a crowd. Not long after, he walks in. He sits down and drinks whiskey with them. Someone posts a photo on LinkedIn.",
"end": 28.706
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 28.706,
"vo_start": 28.981,
"vo_end": 38.339,
"text": "Two years later, US intelligence has concluded she was recruited by China's Ministry of State Security. And according to the New York Times, her assignment was him.",
"end": 38.614
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 38.614,
"vo_start": 38.889,
"vo_end": 50.283,
"text": "Last Sunday, Jensen Huang wrote to every one of his employees: 'I was unfaithful to my wife.' And according to the Times, President Trump was briefed on her about a year before any of it came out. He did nothing with it.",
"end": 50.558
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 50.558,
"vo_start": 50.833,
"vo_end": 56.356,
"text": "This is how China's spy service got next to the most important man in the AI race. And why nobody warned him.",
"end": 56.631
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 56.631,
"vo_start": 56.906,
"vo_end": 62.999,
"text": "This film is built on reporting by the New York Times, the Wall Street Journal and the Financial Times.",
"end": 63.949
}
],
